        "reasoning_summary": "The claim makes two assertions: (1) Alphabet had a large increase in capital expenditures in 2025, and (2) this had no visible effect on profitability. \n\nEvidence E6 shows capital expenditures increased from $38.3 billion (9 months 2024) to $63.6 billion (9 months 2025), representing a substantial increase. Evidence E1 confirms \"We invested heavily in capital expenditures in 2025.\"\n\nHowever, the evidence does not provide actual profitability metrics (net margin, operating margin, or gross margin) for FY2025 or FY2024 that would allow verification of whether profitability was affected. While E1 mentions that margins on certain revenues \"vary significantly and are generally lower than the margins on our advertising revenues\" and discusses expected future cost increases, it does not provide actual margin figures or year-over-year comparisons for 2025 vs 2024.\n\nWithout concrete profitability data for FY2025 and FY2024, I cannot verify whether the capital expenditure increase had \"no visible effect\" on profitability. The evidence establishes the capex increase but is insufficient to verify the profitability claim.",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
